---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const faq = [
  ['What does CleanDraft do?', 'It edits AI-written text so it reads more naturally. It removes stock phrases and filler transitions, swaps stiff words for plain ones, adds contractions, simplifies dashes and varies some sentence openings. It shows every change.'],
  ['Is it an AI tool?', 'No. It is a rule-based editor with no language model. It uses a written list of phrases and rules, and it runs in your browser.'],
  ['Is it free? Is there a limit?', 'It is free with no account. Each run can take up to 10,000 words, and there is no daily cap. The limit exists only to keep your browser responsive.'],
  ['Why did it change so little?', 'CleanDraft is a light editor, not a rewriter. It only changes text where a rule applies. Try Strong, or press Regenerate for an alternative. If your text had few stock phrases, a small edit is the right result.'],
  ['Where does my text go?', 'The tool runs on your device and we never send your text anywhere. We do not store it or use it for training. Ad and analytics scripts can load on the same page, as on any ad-supported site.'],
  ['Will it change my facts?', 'Numbers, dates, names, URLs, quotes and citations are locked, then checked again by a separate checker. An edit that fails that check is undone. Rules can still shift a shade of meaning in other words, so read the edit log before you publish.'],
  ['Will it get past AI detectors?', 'We make no promise about any detector, and we do not tune rules against them. Detector results vary and change often. Our aim is better writing.'],
  ['Can students use it?', 'Check your school rules on AI first. If AI assistance is allowed, editing your own draft is a reasonable use. You are responsible for what you submit. Our guide on disclosing AI use may help.'],
  ['How do I undo one edit?', 'Open the edit log and press "This edit is wrong" on that line. Only that edit goes back. It records which rule misfired, without sending your text.'],
  ['What does Regenerate do?', 'It runs the same rules with a new random seed, which picks different valid replacements. Undo returns to the previous version.'],
  ['Which languages are supported?', 'English only for now, with US, UK, Canadian and Australian spelling.'],
  ['Does it work on my phone?', 'Yes. The site is built for phones first and does not need an app.'],
];
const ld = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })) };
---

<Base title="FAQ: CleanDraft Text Humanizer" description="Answers about CleanDraft: how the rule-based editor works, word limits, where your text goes, what is protected, and what it can't promise." jsonLd={ld}>
  <article class="page">
    <h1>Frequently asked questions</h1>
    {faq.map(([q, a]) => (<><h2>{q}</h2><p>{a}</p></>))}
  </article>
</Base>
